ro Atlanta, could erase Jackson's slim edge.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Brian Kemp (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Georgia's governor's race is a genuine tossup to succeed term-limited Republican Brian Kemp. Democrat Keisha Lance Bottoms, the former Atlanta mayor, faces Republican Rick Jackson in a contest most raters call a tossup, though two rate Democrats slightly ahead. Polling since mid-September shows Jackson narrowly ahead, with margins between one and three points, while prediction markets give Bottoms roughly a 45 to 47 percent chance of winning.\n\nThe defining feature is money: Jackson has raised about $119 million to Bottoms' $5.8 million, and news reports describe a staggering Democratic advertising deficit heading into early voting. A favorable national environment for Democrats and Georgia's near-even partisan lean keep the race tight, but the spending gap and the recent polling trend give Jackson a slim edge. Expect a margin of roughly a point, with real room for movement if the air war or turnout shifts.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6414, "margin": 1.75}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5561, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.5612, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.5894, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5413, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.5396, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.5736, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.463, "margin": -0.7}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.5513, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.5602, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.4361, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.4421, "margin": -1.0}]}